<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Publius Quinctilius Varus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 46 BCE – <a href="/years/9">9 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Publius Quinctilius Varus was a Roman general and governor. In 9 CE his three legions were ambushed and destroyed by Germanic tribes under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>. The defeat ended Roman attempts to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> and fixed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>'s northern frontier along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus.jpg" alt="Publius Quinctilius Varus" width="252" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varus_-_Antiquitates_Germanic%C3%A6_-_Tacitus_%26_Hooft.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Rhine-Danube frontier, fixed after Arminius ambushed three legions under <b>Publius Quinctilius Varus</b> in the Teutoburg Forest, marked the northern limit of Roman expansion, with lasting effects on European history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 9 CE they joined Arminius against <b>Varus</b> and captured the eagle of the Nineteenth Legion, which Germanicus' general Stertinius recovered in 15.</blockquote><a href="/state/bructeri">Bructeri</a></li><li><blockquote>In 9 CE Arminius led a coalition that destroyed the legions of <b>Publius Quinctilius Varus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cherusci">Cherusci</a></li><li><blockquote>His reign began with the killing of some three thousand people when he suppressed a Passover protest in the Temple courts, and further revolts had to be crushed by the Syrian governor <b>Varus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-ethnarchy-of-judea">Herodian Ethnarchy of Judea</a></li><li><blockquote>Maroboduus stayed neutral when Arminius destroyed <b>Varus</b>' legions in 9, and in 17 Arminius defeated him; the Semnones and Langobardi deserted.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-maroboduus">Kingdom of Maroboduus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cremona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.13,10.03&amp;year=-11" aria-label="Cremona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4788 570 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Publius Quinctilius Varus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5238 840h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5238 840h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.13,10.03&amp;year=-11">What was here in 11 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
